  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA 500-hPa trough is currently moving across the central and\nsouthern Plains with an associated surface low pressure system\ndeveloping in the vicinity of the OK/TX panhandles. A stalled\nfrontal boundary is positioned from northern IL to southern KS\nand is acting as this developing system's warm front. Further\nnorth, across northern IA and eastern NE, a cold front is\nadvancing to the southeast from a low pressure system in Canada.\nWeak convergence combined with upper-level support from the \ntrough has resulted in showers forming along the stalled front \nacross northeastern KS and northwestern MO. Further northeast,\nflow along the boundary is much more parallel rather than\nconvergent, hence why showers have not formed further north.\nTemperatures this afternoon are expected to hold in the low-70s.\n\nAs the cold front continues to push southeast, it will collide \nwith the stalled front and both fronts will move southeast \nthrough the CWA. The southeastward movement of the front will \ncreate more convergence, allowing widespread showers to develop\nacross the CWA. With PWATs ahead of the front being in the \n2-2.25\" range (100th percentile for early October), this will \ncreate a setup for efficient, heavy rainfall. With 1-2\" of rain \nhaving already fallen across northern MO this morning, an \nadditional 1-2\" will likely cause flash flooding concerns. With \nthe front moving southeast, the heaviest axis of precipitation \nshould be along and south of the Missouri River. As such, the\nFlood Watch has been extended southward through the full CWA \nthrough 1 AM CDT, but with conditions already being primed for \nflooding, concerns still exist north of this area, particularly \nbefore the front starts moving. Weak instability (500-1000 J/kg) \nis present along and south of the front, which may allow a\nrumble of thunder or two to be possible this afternoon, but \nsevere weather is not expected.\n\nThe cold front, and associated showers, are expected to move out \nof the area early tomorrow morning. Skies will clear behind \nfront as surface high pressure builds in, creating deep layer \nsubsidence. Winds behind front are expected to be a little bit \ngusty (25-30 mph) out of the north-northeast through the morning\ntomorrow before the pressure gradient relaxes. Temperatures \nwill only climb into the mid-to-upper 60s, with lows falling \ninto the mid-to-upper 40s, bringing that Fall feel back to the \narea. Weak mid-level ridging is progged across the \nwestern/central CONUS into the weekend, allowing heights to rise\nand temperatures to return to the 70s. A shortwave is expected \nto move across the northern Plains early next week, bringing \nanother cold front to the area, but precipitation chances are \nnearly 0%. This, along with surface high pressure building back \ninto the area, will cool temperatures slightly, back towards the\n60s, but higher amplitude ridging is forecast to follow. This \nwill result in a warming trend, with temperatures climbing \ntowards the low 80s by Wednesday and Thursday next week.",
